## Test Integrity

Make failing tests pass by changing source code. Do not remove asserts, add skip or xfail, change expected values, or delete tests unless the issue declares the behavior change. If a test is wrong, say so under **Behavior changes** in the PR body with the reason.

Every existing test function you edit gets a **Behavior changes** line naming its pytest node id and the reason — even when the issue itself asks for the change. The merge's held-out gate runs the base copy of each test against your src and fails the merge on any failure no such line names; the issue asking for it does not count. Write one line per test, the node id relative to the project directory, as pytest prints it:

```
- tests/test_example.py::test_name — why the old expectation no longer holds
- tests/test_example.py::TestGroup::test_case — why
```

A parametrized test's line may name the test or each failing case (`tests/test_example.py::test_name[case]`). Tests you only add need no line. A base test file that fails to collect against your src (an import your change broke) takes one line naming the file (`- tests/test_example.py — why`): it declares the tests of that file that never ran, never one that ran and failed.

Write the lines now, not after a red merge: the same held-out gate can also run before review, on your head merged with the current base, and then any base test that fails with no line naming it comes back to you as a remediation round before a reviewer reads the PR.

An existing frontend test file (`*.test.ts`, `*.test.tsx`, `scripts/ui-checks/`) has no node ids: if the diff changes one in any way, adding cases, deleting or renaming it included, write a line naming its path on the base branch (`- frontend/src/lib/example.test.ts — why`). A new frontend test file needs no line.
{% if host_publishes %}

Write these lines in your Dispatch Report's **Behavior changes** section. They are proposals, not declarations: the PR body shows them under **Proposed behavior changes**, which the held-out gate does not read. kew's reviewer rules on each line in its verdict, and kew adds the lines it accepts to the PR's **Behavior changes** section; a line it rejects comes back to you as a finding with the reviewer's reason. Give each line a reason the issue supports.
